 -- Function: mean_gamma (A, B)
     Returns the mean of a \Gamma\left(a,b\right) random variable, with
     a,b>0.  To make use of this function, write first
     ‘load("distrib")’.

     The mean is

     E[X] = ab


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function mean_general_finite_discrete v,  Next: Function mean_geometric p,  Prev: Function mean_gamma a b,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: mean_general_finite_discrete (V)
     Returns the mean of a general finite discrete random variable, with
     vector probabilities v.

     See ‘pdf_general_finite_discrete’ for more details.

     See also: ‘pdf_general_finite_discrete’.


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function mean_geometric p,  Next: Function mean_gumbel a b,  Prev: Function mean_general_finite_discrete v,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: mean_geometric (P)
     Returns the mean of a {\it Geometric}(p) random variable, with 0 <
     p \leq 1.

     The mean is

     E[X] = {1\over p} - 1

     The probability from which the mean is derived is defined as p (1 -
     p)^x.  This is interpreted as the probability of x failures before
     the first success.

     ‘load("distrib")’ loads this function.


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function mean_gumbel a b,  Next: Function mean_hypergeometric n_1 n_2 n,  Prev: Function mean_geometric p,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: mean_gumbel (A, B)
     Returns the mean of a {\it Gumbel}(a,b) random variable, with b>0.

     The mean is

     E[X] = a+b\gamma

     (%i1) load ("distrib")$
     
     (%i2) mean_gumbel(a,b);
     (%o2)                     %gamma b + a

     where symbol ‘%gamma’ stands for the Euler-Mascheroni constant.
     See also ‘%gamma’.

     See also: ‘%gamma’.


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function mean_hypergeometric n_1 n_2 n,  Next: Function mean_laplace a b,  Prev: Function mean_gumbel a b,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: mean_hypergeometric (N_1, N_2, N)
     Returns the mean of a discrete uniform random variable {\it
     Hypergeometric}(n_1,n_2,n) , with n_1, n_2 and n non negative
     integers and n\leq n_1+n_2.  To make use of this function, write
     first ‘load("distrib")’.

     The mean is

     E[X] = {n n_1\over n_2+n_1}


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function mean_laplace a b,  Next: Function mean_logistic a b,  Prev: Function mean_hypergeometric n_1 n_2 n,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: mean_laplace (A, B)
     Returns the mean of a {\it Laplace}(a,b) random variable, with b>0.
     To make use of this function, write first ‘load("distrib")’.

     The mean is

     E[X] = a


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function mean_logistic a b,  Next: Function mean_lognormal m s,  Prev: Function mean_laplace a b,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: mean_logistic (A, B)
     Returns the mean of a {\it Logistic}(a,b) random variable , with
     b>0.  To make use of this function, write first ‘load("distrib")’.

     The mean is

     E[X] = a


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function mean_lognormal m s,  Next: Function mean_negative_binomial n p,  Prev: Function mean_logistic a b,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: mean_lognormal (M, S)
     Returns the mean of a {\it Lognormal}(m,s) random variable, with
     s>0.  To make use of this function, write first ‘load("distrib")’.

     The mean is

     E[X] = \exp\left(m+{s^2\over 2}\right)


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function mean_negative_binomial n p,  Next: Function mean_noncentral_chi2 n ncp,  Prev: Function mean_lognormal m s,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: mean_negative_binomial (N, P)
     Returns the mean of a {\it NegativeBinomial}(n,p) random variable,
     with 0 < p \leq 1 and n a positive number.  To make use of this
     function, write first ‘load("distrib")’.

     The mean is

     E[X] = {n(1-p)\over p}


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function mean_noncentral_chi2 n ncp,  Next: Function mean_noncentral_student_t n ncp,  Prev: Function mean_negative_binomial n p,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: mean_noncentral_chi2 (N, NCP)
     Returns the mean of a noncentral Chi-square random variable
     m4_noncentral_chi2(n,ncp) , with n>0 and noncentrality parameter
     ncp \ge 0.

     The mean is

     E[X] = n + \mu

     where \mu is the noncentrality parameter _ncp_.


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function mean_noncentral_student_t n ncp,  Next: Function mean_normal m s,  Prev: Function mean_noncentral_chi2 n ncp,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: mean_noncentral_student_t (N, NCP)
     Returns the mean of a noncentral Student random variable {\it
     nc\_t}(n, ncp) , with n>1 degrees of freedom and noncentrality
     parameter ncp.  To make use of this function, write first
     ‘load("distrib")’.

     The mean is

     E[X] = {\mu \sqrt{n}\; \Gamma\left(\displaystyle{n-1\over 2}\right)
     \over \sqrt{2}\;\Gamma\left(\displaystyle{n\over 2}\right)}

     where \mu is the noncentrality parameter ncp.

     (%i1) load ("distrib")$
     
     (%i2) mean_noncentral_student_t(df,k);
                               df - 1
                         gamma(------) sqrt(df) k
                                 2
     (%o2)               ------------------------
                                          df
                            sqrt(2) gamma(--)
                                          2


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function mean_normal m s,  Next: Function mean_pareto a b,  Prev: Function mean_noncentral_student_t n ncp,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: mean_normal (M, S)
     Returns the mean of a {\it Normal}(m, s) random variable, with s>0.
     To make use of this function, write first ‘load("distrib")’.

     The mean is

     E[X] = m


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function mean_pareto a b,  Next: Function mean_poisson m,  Prev: Function mean_normal m s,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: mean_pareto (A, B)
     Returns the mean of a {\it Pareto}(a,b) random variable, with
     a>1,b>0.  To make use of this function, write first
     ‘load("distrib")’.

     The mean is

     E[X] = {ab\over a-1}


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function mean_poisson m,  Next: Function mean_rayleigh b,  Prev: Function mean_pareto a b,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: mean_poisson (M)
     Returns the mean of a {\it Poisson}(m) random variable, with m>0.
     To make use of this function, write first ‘load("distrib")’.

     The mean is

     E[X] = m


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function mean_rayleigh b,  Next: Function mean_student_t n,  Prev: Function mean_poisson m,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: mean_rayleigh (B)
     Returns the mean of a {\it Rayleigh}(b) random variable, with b>0.

     The {\it Rayleigh}(b) random variable is equivalent to the {\it
     Weibull}(2,1/b) .

     The mean is

     E[X] = {\sqrt{\pi}\over 2b}

     (%i1) load ("distrib")$
     
     (%i2) mean_rayleigh(b);
                                 sqrt(%pi)
     (%o2)                       ---------
                                    2 b


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function mean_student_t n,  Next: Function mean_weibull a b,  Prev: Function mean_rayleigh b,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: mean_student_t (N)
     Returns the mean of a Student random variable t(n) , with n>0.  To
     make use of this function, write first ‘load("distrib")’.

     The mean is

     E[X] = 0


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function mean_weibull a b,  Next: Function pdf_bernoulli x p,  Prev: Function mean_student_t n,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: mean_weibull (A, B)
     Returns the mean of a {\it Weibull}(a,b) random variable, with
     a,b>0.  To make use of this function, write first
     ‘load("distrib")’.

     The mean is

     E[X] = b\Gamma\left(1+{1\over a}\right)


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function pdf_bernoulli x p,  Next: Function pdf_beta x a b,  Prev: Function mean_weibull a b,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: pdf_bernoulli (X, P)
     Returns the value at _x_ of the probability function of a {\it
     Bernoulli}(p) random variable, with 0 \leq p \leq 1.

     The {\it Bernoulli}(p) random variable is equivalent to the {\it
     Binomial}(1,p) .

     The mean is

     f(x; p) = p^x (1-p)^{1-x}

     (%i1) load ("distrib")$
     
     (%i2) pdf_bernoulli(1,p);
     (%o2) if equal(p, 0) then 0 elseif equal(p, 1) then 1 else p


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function pdf_beta x a b,  Next: Function pdf_binomial x n p,  Prev: Function pdf_bernoulli x p,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: pdf_beta (X, A, B)
     Returns the value at _x_ of the density function of a {\it
     Beta}(a,b) random variable, with a,b>0.  To make use of this
     function, write first ‘load("distrib")’.

     The pdf is

     f(x; a, b) = \begin{cases} \displaystyle{x^{a-1}(1-x)^{b-1} \over
     B(a,b)} & for $0 \le x \le 1$ \cr \cr 0 & otherwise \end{cases}


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function pdf_binomial x n p,  Next: Function pdf_cauchy x a b,  Prev: Function pdf_beta x a b,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: pdf_binomial (X, N, P)
     Returns the value at _x_ of the probability function of a {\it
     Binomial}(n,p) random variable, with 0 \leq p \leq 1 and n a
     positive integer.  To make use of this function, write first
     ‘load("distrib")’.

     The pdf is

     f(x; n, p) = {n\choose x} (1-p)^{n-x}p^x


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function pdf_cauchy x a b,  Next: Function pdf_chi2 x n,  Prev: Function pdf_binomial x n p,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: pdf_cauchy (X, A, B)
     Returns the value at _x_ of the density function of a {\it
     Cauchy}(a,b) random variable, with b>0.  To make use of this
     function, write first ‘load("distrib")’.

     The pdf is

     f(x; a, b) = {b\over \pi\left((x-a)^2+b^2\right)}


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function pdf_chi2 x n,  Next: Function pdf_continuous_uniform x a b,  Prev: Function pdf_cauchy x a b,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: pdf_chi2 (X, N)
     Returns the value at _x_ of the density function of a Chi-square
     random variable \chi^2(n) , with n>0.  The \chi^2(n) random
     variable is equivalent to the \Gamma\left(n/2,2\right) .

     The pdf is

     f(x; n) = \begin{cases} \displaystyle{x^{n/2-1} e^{-x/2} \over
     2^{n/2} \Gamma\left(\displaystyle{n\over 2}\right)} & for $x > 0$
     \cr \cr 0 & otherwise \end{cases}

     (%i1) load ("distrib")$
     
     (%i2) pdf_chi2(x,n);
                         - x/2  n/2 - 1
                       %e      x        unit_step(x)
     (%o2)             -----------------------------
                                n/2       n
                               2    gamma(-)
                                          2


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function pdf_continuous_uniform x a b,  Next: Function pdf_discrete_uniform x n,  Prev: Function pdf_chi2 x n,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: pdf_continuous_uniform (X, A, B)
     Returns the value at _x_ of the density function of a {\it
     ContinuousUniform}(a,b) random variable, with a \lt b. To make use
     of this function, write first ‘load("distrib")’.

     The pdf

     f(x; a, b) = \begin{cases} \displaystyle{1\over b-a} & for $0 \le x
     \le 1$ \cr \cr 0 & otherwise \end{cases}

     and is 0 otherwise.


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function pdf_discrete_uniform x n,  Next: Function pdf_exp x m,  Prev: Function pdf_continuous_uniform x a b,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: pdf_discrete_uniform (X, N)
     Returns the value at _x_ of the probability function of a {\it
     DiscreteUniform}(n) random variable, with n a strictly positive
     integer.  To make use of this function, write first
     ‘load("distrib")’.

     The pdf is

     f(x,n) = {1\over n}


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function pdf_exp x m,  Next: Function pdf_f x m n,  Prev: Function pdf_discrete_uniform x n,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: pdf_exp (X, M)
     Returns the value at _x_ of the density function of an {\it
     Exponential}(m) random variable, with m>0.

     The {\it Exponential}(m) random variable is equivalent to the {\it
     Weibull}(1,1/m) .

     The pdf is

     f(x; m) = \begin{cases} me^{-mx} & for $x \ge 0$ \cr 0 & otherwise
     \end{cases}

     (%i1) load ("distrib")$
     
     (%i2) pdf_exp(x,m);
                            - m x
     (%o2)                %e      m unit_step(x)


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function pdf_f x m n,  Next: Function pdf_gamma x a b,  Prev: Function pdf_exp x m,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: pdf_f (X, M, N)
     Returns the value at _x_ of the density function of a F random
     variable F(m,n), with m,n>0.  To make use of this function, write
     first ‘load("distrib")’.

     The pdf is

     f(x; m, n) = \begin{cases} B\left(\displaystyle{m\over 2},
     \displaystyle{n\over 2}\right)^{-1} \left(\displaystyle{m\over
     n}\right)^{m/ 2} x^{m/2-1} \left(1 + \displaystyle{m\over
     n}x\right)^{-\left(n+m\right)/2} & $x > 0$ \cr \cr 0 & otherwise
     \end{cases}


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function pdf_gamma x a b,  Next: Function pdf_general_finite_discrete x v,  Prev: Function pdf_f x m n,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: pdf_gamma (X, A, B)
     Returns the value at _x_ of the density function of a
     \Gamma\left(a,b\right) random variable, with a,b>0.  To make use of
     this function, write first ‘load("distrib")’.

     The shape parameter is a, and the scale parameter is b.

     The pdf is

     f(x; a, b) = {x^{a-1}e^{-x/b}\over b^a \Gamma(a)}


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function pdf_general_finite_discrete x v,  Next: Function pdf_geometric x p,  Prev: Function pdf_gamma x a b,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: pdf_general_finite_discrete (X, V)
     Returns the value at _x_ of the probability function of a general
     finite discrete random variable, with vector probabilities v, such
     that Pr(X=i) = v_i.  Vector v can be a list of nonnegative
     expressions whose components will be normalized to get a vector of
     probabilities.  To make use of this function, write first
     ‘load("distrib")’.

     Note that i=1 corresponds to the first element of v.

     (%i1) load ("distrib")$
     
     (%i2) pdf_general_finite_discrete(2, [1/7, 4/7, 2/7]);
                                     4
     (%o2)                           -
                                     7
     
     
     (%i3) pdf_general_finite_discrete(2, [1, 4, 2]);
                                     4
     (%o3)                           -
                                     7


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function pdf_geometric x p,  Next: Function pdf_gumbel x a b,  Prev: Function pdf_general_finite_discrete x v,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: pdf_geometric (X, P)
     Returns the value at _x_ of the probability function of a {\it
     Geometric}(p) random variable, with 0 < p \leq 1

     The pdf is

     f(x; p) = p(1-p)^x

     This is interpreted as the probability of x failures before the
     first success.

     ‘load("distrib")’ loads this function.


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function pdf_gumbel x a b,  Next: Function pdf_hypergeometric x n_1 n_2 n,  Prev: Function pdf_geometric x p,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: pdf_gumbel (X, A, B)
     Returns the value at _x_ of the density function of a {\it
     Gumbel}(a,b) random variable, with b>0.  To make use of this
     function, write first ‘load("distrib")’.

     The pdf is

     f(x; a, b) = {1\over b} \exp\left[{a-x\over b} -
     \exp\left({a-x\over b}\right)\right]


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function pdf_hypergeometric x n_1 n_2 n,  Next: Function pdf_laplace x a b,  Prev: Function pdf_gumbel x a b,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: pdf_hypergeometric (X, N_1, N_2, N)
     Returns the value at _x_ of the probability function of a {\it
     Hypergeometric}(n1,n2,n) random variable, with n_1, n_2 and n non
     negative integers and n\leq n_1+n_2.  Being n_1 the number of
     objects of class A, n_2 the number of objects of class B, and n the
     size of the sample without replacement, this function returns the
     probability of event "exactly _x_ objects are of class A".

     To make use of this function, write first ‘load("distrib")’.

     The pdf is

     f(x; n_1, n_2, n) = {\displaystyle{n_1\choose x} {n_2 \choose n-x}
     \over \displaystyle{n_2+n_1 \choose n}}


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function pdf_laplace x a b,  Next: Function pdf_logistic x a b,  Prev: Function pdf_hypergeometric x n_1 n_2 n,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: pdf_laplace (X, A, B)
     Returns the value at _x_ of the density function of a {\it
     Laplace}(a,b) random variable, with b>0.  To make use of this
     function, write first ‘load("distrib")’.

     Here, a is the location parameter (or mean), and b is the scale
     parameter, related to the variance.

     The pdf is

     f(x; a, b) = {1\over 2b}\exp\left(-{|x-a|\over b}\right)


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function pdf_logistic x a b,  Next: Function pdf_lognormal x m s,  Prev: Function pdf_laplace x a b,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: pdf_logistic (X, A, B)
     Returns the value at _x_ of the density function of a {\it
     Logistic}(a,b) random variable , with b>0.  To make use of this
     function, write first ‘load("distrib")’.

     a is the location parameter and b is the scale parameter.

     The pdf is

     f(x; a, b) = {e^{-(x-a)/b} \over b\left(1 + e^{-(x-a)/b}\right)^2}


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function pdf_lognormal x m s,  Next: Function pdf_negative_binomial x n p,  Prev: Function pdf_logistic x a b,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: pdf_lognormal (X, M, S)
     Returns the value at _x_ of the density function of a {\it
     Lognormal}(m,s) random variable, with s>0.  To make use of this
     function, write first ‘load("distrib")’.

     The pdf is

     f(x; m, s) = \begin{cases} \displaystyle{1\over x s \sqrt{2\pi}}
     \exp\left(-\displaystyle{\left(\log x - m\right)^2\over
     2s^2}\right) & for $x \ge 0$ \cr \cr 0 & for $x < 0$ \end{cases}


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function pdf_negative_binomial x n p,  Next: Function pdf_noncentral_chi2 x n ncp,  Prev: Function pdf_lognormal x m s,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: pdf_negative_binomial (X, N, P)
     Returns the value at _x_ of the probability function of a {\it
     NegativeBinomial}(n,p) random variable, with 0 < p \leq 1 and n a
     positive number.  To make use of this function, write first
     ‘load("distrib")’.

     The pdf is

     f(x; n, p) = {x+n-1 \choose n-1} (1-p)^xp^n


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function pdf_noncentral_chi2 x n ncp,  Next: Function pdf_noncentral_student_t x n ncp,  Prev: Function pdf_negative_binomial x n p,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: pdf_noncentral_chi2 (X, N, NCP)
     Returns the value at x of the density function of a noncentral
     \chi^2 random variable m4_noncentral_chi2(n,ncp) , with n>0 and
     noncentrality parameter ncp \ge 0. To make use of this function,
     write first ‘load("distrib")’.

     For x < 0, the pdf is 0, and for x \ge 0 the pdf is

     f(x; n, \lambda) = {1\over 2}e^{-(x+\lambda)/2} \left(x\over
     \lambda\right)^{n/4-1/2}I_{{n\over 2} - 1}\left(\sqrt{n
     \lambda}\right)


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function pdf_noncentral_student_t x n ncp,  Next: Function pdf_normal x m s,  Prev: Function pdf_noncentral_chi2 x n ncp,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: pdf_noncentral_student_t (X, N, NCP)
     Returns the value at _x_ of the density function of a noncentral
     Student random variable {\it nc\_t}(n, ncp) , with n>0 degrees of
     freedom and noncentrality parameter ncp.  To make use of this
     function, write first ‘load("distrib")’.

     The pdf is

     f(x; n, \mu) = \left[\sqrt{n} B\left({1\over 2}, {n\over
     2}\right)\right]^{-1}\left(1+{x^2\over n}\right)^{-{(n+1)/2}}
     e^{-\mu^2/ 2} \bigg[A_n(x; \mu) + B_n(x; \mu)\bigg]

     where

     \begin{aligned} A_n(x;\mu) &= {}_1F_1\left({n+1\over 2}; {1\over
     2}; {\mu^2 x^2\over 2\left(x^2+n\right)}\right) \cr B_n(x;\mu) &=
     {\sqrt{2}\mu x \over \sqrt{x^2+n}} {\Gamma\left({n\over 2} +
     1\right)\over \Gamma\left({n+1\over 2}\right)}\;
     {}_1F_1\left({n\over 2} + 1; {3\over 2}; {\mu^2 x^2\over
     2\left(x^2+n\right)}\right) \end{aligned}

     and \mu is the non-centrality parameter ncp.

     Sometimes an extra work is necessary to get the final result.

     (%i1) load ("distrib")$
     
     (%i2) expand(pdf_noncentral_student_t(3,5,0.1));
     rat: replaced 0.018898223650461364 by 15934951/843198350 = 0.018898223650461364
     
     rat: replaced -8.734356480209641 by -294697965/33740089 = -8.734356480209641
     
     rat: replaced 4.136255165816327 by 51033443/12338079 = 4.136255165816332
     
     rat: replaced 1.0806143216420299 by 49366521/45683756 = 1.0806143216420296
     
     rat: replaced 0.0565127306411839 by 5608717/99246965 = 0.05651273064118384
     
     rat: replaced -300.8069396896258 by -79782423/265228 = -300.80693968962555
     
     rat: replaced 160.62691761849732 by 178374907/1110492 = 160.62691761849703
                                 7/2                          7/2
           0.042964144174009046 5      1.3236503072892878e-6 5
     (%o2) ------------------------- + --------------------------
              3/2   5/2                        sqrt(%pi)
             2    14    sqrt(%pi)
                                                                   7/2
                                              1.94793720435093e-4 5
                                            + ------------------------
                                                        %pi
     
     
     (%i3) float(%);
     (%o3)                 0.020805931594056706


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function pdf_normal x m s,  Next: Function pdf_pareto x a b,  Prev: Function pdf_noncentral_student_t x n ncp,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: pdf_normal (X, M, S)
     Returns the value at _x_ of the density function of a {\it
     Normal}(m, s) random variable, with s>0.  To make use of this
     function, write first ‘load("distrib")’.

     The pdf is

     f(x; m, s) = {1\over s\sqrt{2\pi}} e^{\displaystyle -{(x-m)^2\over
     2s^2}}


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function pdf_pareto x a b,  Next: Function pdf_poisson x m,  Prev: Function pdf_normal x m s,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: pdf_pareto (X, A, B)
     Returns the value at _x_ of the density function of a {\it
     Pareto}(a,b) random variable, with a,b>0.  To make use of this
     function, write first ‘load("distrib")’.

     The pdf is

     f(x; a, b) = \begin{cases} \displaystyle{a b^a \over x^{a+1}} & for
     $x \ge b$ \cr \cr 0 & for $x < b$ \end{cases}


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function pdf_poisson x m,  Next: Function pdf_rayleigh x b,  Prev: Function pdf_pareto x a b,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: pdf_poisson (X, M)
     Returns the value at _x_ of the probability function of a {\it
     Poisson}(m) random variable, with m>0.  To make use of this
     function, write first ‘load("distrib")’.

     The pdf is

     f(x; m) = {m^x e^{-m}\over x!}


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function pdf_rayleigh x b,  Next: Function pdf_student_t x n,  Prev: Function pdf_poisson x m,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: pdf_rayleigh (X, B)
     Returns the value at _x_ of the density function of a {\it
     Rayleigh}(b) random variable, with b>0.

     The {\it Rayleigh}(b) random variable is equivalent to the {\it
     Weibull}(2,1/b) .

     The pdf is

     f(x; b) = \begin{cases} 2b^2 x e^{-b^2 x^2} & for $x \ge 0$ \cr 0 &
     for $x < 0$ \end{cases}

     (%i1) load ("distrib")$
     
     (%i2) pdf_rayleigh(x,b);
                              2  2
                           - b  x   2
     (%o2)             2 %e        b  x unit_step(x)


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function pdf_student_t x n,  Next: Function pdf_weibull x a b,  Prev: Function pdf_rayleigh x b,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: pdf_student_t (X, N)
     Returns the value at _x_ of the density function of a Student
     random variable t(n) , with n>0 degrees of freedom.  To make use of
     this function, write first ‘load("distrib")’.

     The pdf is

     f(x; n) = \left[\sqrt{n} B\left({1\over 2}, {n\over
     2}\right)\right]^{-1} \left(1+{x^2\over n}\right)^{\displaystyle
     -{n+1\over 2}}


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function pdf_weibull x a b,  Next: Function quantile_bernoulli q p,  Prev: Function pdf_student_t x n,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: pdf_weibull (X, A, B)
     Returns the value at _x_ of the density function of a {\it
     Weibull}(a,b) random variable, with a,b>0.  To make use of this
     function, write first ‘load("distrib")’.

     The pdf is

     f(x; a, b) = \begin{cases} \displaystyle{1\over b} \left({x\over
     b}\right)^{a-1} e^{-(x/b)^a} & for $x \ge 0$ \cr \cr 0 & for $x <
     0$ \end{cases}


