 -- Function: quantile_weibull (Q, A, B)
     Returns the _q_-quantile of a {\it Weibull}(a,b) random variable,
     with a,b>0; in other words, this is the inverse of ‘cdf_weibull’.
     Argument _q_ must be an element of [0,1].  To make use of this
     function, write first ‘load("distrib")’.


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function random_bernoulli p,  Next: Function random_beta a b,  Prev: Function quantile_weibull q a b,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: random_bernoulli (P)
     Returns a {\it Bernoulli}(p) random variate, with 0 \leq p \leq 1.
     Calling ‘random_bernoulli’ with a second argument _n_, a random
     sample of size _n_ will be simulated.

     This is a direct application of the ‘random’ built-in Maxima
     function.

     See also ‘random’.  To make use of this function, write first
     ‘load("distrib")’.

     See also: ‘random’.


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function random_beta a b,  Next: Function random_binomial n p,  Prev: Function random_bernoulli p,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: random_beta (A, B)
     Returns a {\it Beta}(a,b) random variate, with a,b>0.  Calling
     ‘random_beta’ with a third argument _n_, a random sample of size
     _n_ will be simulated.

     The implemented algorithm is defined in Cheng, R.C.H. (1978).
     _Generating Beta Variates with Nonintegral Shape Parameters_.
     Communications of the ACM, 21:317-322

     To make use of this function, write first ‘load("distrib")’.


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function random_binomial n p,  Next: Function random_cauchy a b,  Prev: Function random_beta a b,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: random_binomial (N, P)
     Returns a {\it Binomial}(n,p) random variate, with 0 \leq p \leq 1
     and n a positive integer.  Calling ‘random_binomial’ with a third
     argument _m_, a random sample of size _m_ will be simulated.

     The implemented algorithm is based on the one described in
     Kachitvichyanukul, V. and Schmeiser, B.W. (1988) _Binomial Random
     Variate Generation_.  Communications of the ACM, 31, Feb., 216.

     To make use of this function, write first ‘load("distrib")’.


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function random_cauchy a b,  Next: Function random_chi2 n,  Prev: Function random_binomial n p,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: random_cauchy (A, B)
     Returns a {\it Cauchy}(a,b) random variate, with b>0.  Calling
     ‘random_cauchy’ with a third argument _n_, a random sample of size
     _n_ will be simulated.

     The implemented algorithm is based on the general inverse method.

     To make use of this function, write first ‘load("distrib")’.


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function random_chi2 n,  Next: Function random_continuous_uniform a b,  Prev: Function random_cauchy a b,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: random_chi2 (N)
     Returns a Chi-square random variate \chi^2(n) , with n>0.  Calling
     ‘random_chi2’ with a second argument _m_, a random sample of size
     _m_ will be simulated.

     The simulation is based on the Ahrens-Cheng algorithm.  See
     ‘random_gamma’ for details.

     To make use of this function, write first ‘load("distrib")’.


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function random_continuous_uniform a b,  Next: Function random_discrete_uniform n,  Prev: Function random_chi2 n,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: random_continuous_uniform (A, B)
     Returns a {\it ContinuousUniform}(a,b) random variate, with a \lt
     b. Calling ‘random_continuous_uniform’ with a third argument _n_, a
     random sample of size _n_ will be simulated.

     This is a direct application of the ‘random’ built-in Maxima
     function.

     See also ‘random’.  To make use of this function, write first
     ‘load("distrib")’.

     See also: ‘random’.


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function random_discrete_uniform n,  Next: Function random_exp m,  Prev: Function random_continuous_uniform a b,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: random_discrete_uniform (N)
     Returns a {\it DiscreteUniform}(n) random variate, with n a
     strictly positive integer.  Calling ‘random_discrete_uniform’ with
     a second argument _m_, a random sample of size _m_ will be
     simulated.

     This is a direct application of the ‘random’ built-in Maxima
     function.

     See also ‘random’.  To make use of this function, write first
     ‘load("distrib")’.

     See also: ‘random’.


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function random_exp m,  Next: Function random_f m n,  Prev: Function random_discrete_uniform n,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: random_exp (M)
     Returns an {\it Exponential}(m) random variate, with m>0.  Calling
     ‘random_exp’ with a second argument _k_, a random sample of size
     _k_ will be simulated.

     The simulation algorithm is based on the general inverse method.

     To make use of this function, write first ‘load("distrib")’.


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function random_f m n,  Next: Function random_gamma a b,  Prev: Function random_exp m,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: random_f (M, N)
     Returns a F random variate F(m,n), with m,n>0.  Calling ‘random_f’
     with a third argument _k_, a random sample of size _k_ will be
     simulated.

     The simulation algorithm is based on the fact that if _X_ is a
     Chi^2(m) random variable and Y is a \chi^2(n) random variable, then

     F={{n X}\over{m Y}}

     is a F random variable with _m_ and _n_ degrees of freedom, F(m,n).

     To make use of this function, write first ‘load("distrib")’.


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function random_gamma a b,  Next: Function random_general_finite_discrete v,  Prev: Function random_f m n,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: random_gamma (A, B)
     Returns a \Gamma\left(a,b\right) random variate, with a,b>0.
     Calling ‘random_gamma’ with a third argument _n_, a random sample
     of size _n_ will be simulated.

     The implemented algorithm is a combination of two procedures,
     depending on the value of parameter _a_:

     For a \ge 1, Cheng, R.C.H. and Feast, G.M. (1979).  _Some simple
     gamma variate generators_.  Appl.  Stat., 28, 3, 290-295.

     For 0 \lt a \lt 1, Ahrens, J.H. and Dieter, U. (1974).  _Computer
     methods for sampling from gamma, , poisson and binomial
     distributions_.  Computing, 12, 223-246.

     To make use of this function, write first ‘load("distrib")’.


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function random_general_finite_discrete v,  Next: Function random_geometric p,  Prev: Function random_gamma a b,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: random_general_finite_discrete (V)
     Returns a general finite discrete random variate, with vector
     probabilities v.  Calling ‘random_general_finite_discrete’ with a
     second argument _m_, a random sample of size _m_ will be simulated.

     See ‘pdf_general_finite_discrete’ for more details.

     (%i1) load ("distrib")$
     
     (%i2) random_general_finite_discrete([1,3,1,5]);
     (%o2)                           4
     
     
     (%i3) random_general_finite_discrete([1,3,1,5], 10);
     (%o3)            [4, 4, 2, 4, 2, 2, 4, 2, 2, 4]

     See also: ‘pdf_general_finite_discrete’.


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function random_geometric p,  Next: Function random_gumbel a b,  Prev: Function random_general_finite_discrete v,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: random_geometric (P)
     ‘random_geometric(p)’ returns one random sample from a {\it
     Geometric}(p) distribution, with 0 \lt p \le 1.

     ‘random_geometric(p, n)’ returns a list of _n_ random samples.

     The algorithm is based on simulation of Bernoulli trials.

     The probability from which the random sample is derived is defined
     as p (1 - p)^x.  This is interpreted as the probability of x
     failures before the first success.

     ‘load("distrib")’ loads this function.


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function random_gumbel a b,  Next: Function random_hypergeometric n1 n2 n,  Prev: Function random_geometric p,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: random_gumbel (A, B)
     Returns a {\it Gumbel}(a,b) random variate, with b>0.  Calling
     ‘random_gumbel’ with a third argument _n_, a random sample of size
     _n_ will be simulated.

     The implemented algorithm is based on the general inverse method.

     To make use of this function, write first ‘load("distrib")’.


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function random_hypergeometric n1 n2 n,  Next: Function random_laplace a b,  Prev: Function random_gumbel a b,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: random_hypergeometric (N1, N2, N)
     Returns a {\it Hypergeometric}(n1,n2,n) random variate, with _n1_,
     _n2_ and _n_ non negative integers and n \le n_1 + n_2. Calling
     ‘random_hypergeometric’ with a fourth argument _m_, a random sample
     of size _m_ will be simulated.

     Algorithm described in Kachitvichyanukul, V., Schmeiser, B.W.
     (1985) _Computer generation of hypergeometric random variates._
     Journal of Statistical Computation and Simulation 22, 127-145.

     To make use of this function, write first ‘load("distrib")’.


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function random_laplace a b,  Next: Function random_logistic a b,  Prev: Function random_hypergeometric n1 n2 n,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: random_laplace (A, B)
     Returns a {\it Laplace}(a,b) random variate, with b>0.  Calling
     ‘random_laplace’ with a third argument _n_, a random sample of size
     _n_ will be simulated.

     The implemented algorithm is based on the general inverse method.

     To make use of this function, write first ‘load("distrib")’.


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function random_logistic a b,  Next: Function random_lognormal m s,  Prev: Function random_laplace a b,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: random_logistic (A, B)
     Returns a {\it Logistic}(a,b) random variate, with b>0.  Calling
     ‘random_logistic’ with a third argument _n_, a random sample of
     size _n_ will be simulated.

     The implemented algorithm is based on the general inverse method.

     To make use of this function, write first ‘load("distrib")’.


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function random_lognormal m s,  Next: Function random_negative_binomial n p,  Prev: Function random_logistic a b,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: random_lognormal (M, S)
     Returns a {\it Lognormal}(m,s) random variate, with s>0.  Calling
     ‘random_lognormal’ with a third argument _n_, a random sample of
     size _n_ will be simulated.

     Log-normal variates are simulated by means of random normal
     variates.  See ‘random_normal’ for details.

     To make use of this function, write first ‘load("distrib")’.


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function random_negative_binomial n p,  Next: Function random_noncentral_chi2 n ncp,  Prev: Function random_lognormal m s,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: random_negative_binomial (N, P)
     Returns a {\it NegativeBinomial}(n,p) random variate, with 0 < p
     \leq 1 and n a positive number.  Calling ‘random_negative_binomial’
     with a third argument _m_, a random sample of size _m_ will be
     simulated.

     Algorithm described in Devroye, L. (1986) _Non-Uniform Random
     Variate Generation_.  Springer Verlag, p. 480.

     To make use of this function, write first ‘load("distrib")’.


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function random_noncentral_chi2 n ncp,  Next: Function random_noncentral_student_t n ncp,  Prev: Function random_negative_binomial n p,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: random_noncentral_chi2 (N, NCP)
     Returns a noncentral Chi-square random variate
     m4_noncentral_chi2(n,ncp) , with n>0 and noncentrality parameter
     ncp \ge 0. Calling ‘random_noncentral_chi2’ with a third argument
     _m_, a random sample of size _m_ will be simulated.

     To make use of this function, write first ‘load("distrib")’.


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function random_noncentral_student_t n ncp,  Next: Function random_normal m s,  Prev: Function random_noncentral_chi2 n ncp,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: random_noncentral_student_t (N, NCP)
     Returns a noncentral Student random variate {\it nc\_t}(n, ncp) ,
     with n>0.  Calling ‘random_noncentral_student_t’ with a third
     argument _m_, a random sample of size _m_ will be simulated.

     The implemented algorithm is based on the fact that if _X_ is a
     normal random variable {\it Normal}(ncp, 1) and S^2 is a \chi^2
     random variable with _n_ degrees of freedom, \chi^2(n) , then

     U={{X}\over{\sqrt{{S^2}\over{n}}}}

     is a noncentral Student random variable with n degrees of freedom
     and noncentrality parameter ncp, {\it nc\_t}(n, ncp) .

     To make use of this function, write first ‘load("distrib")’.


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function random_normal m s,  Next: Function random_pareto a b,  Prev: Function random_noncentral_student_t n ncp,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: random_normal (M, S)
     Returns a {\it Normal}(m, s) random variate, with s>0.  Calling
     ‘random_normal’ with a third argument _n_, a random sample of size
     _n_ will be simulated.

     This is an implementation of the Box-Mueller algorithm, as
     described in Knuth, D.E. (1981) _Seminumerical Algorithms.  The Art
     of Computer Programming._  Addison-Wesley.

     To make use of this function, write first ‘load("distrib")’.


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function random_pareto a b,  Next: Function random_poisson m,  Prev: Function random_normal m s,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: random_pareto (A, B)
     Returns a {\it Pareto}(a,b) random variate, with a>0,b>0.  Calling
     ‘random_pareto’ with a third argument _n_, a random sample of size
     _n_ will be simulated.

     The implemented algorithm is based on the general inverse method.

     To make use of this function, write first ‘load("distrib")’.


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function random_poisson m,  Next: Function random_rayleigh b,  Prev: Function random_pareto a b,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: random_poisson (M)
     Returns a {\it Poisson}(m) random variate, with m>0.  Calling
     ‘random_poisson’ with a second argument _n_, a random sample of
     size _n_ will be simulated.

     The implemented algorithm is the one described in Ahrens, J.H. and
     Dieter, U. (1982) _Computer Generation of Poisson Deviates From
     Modified Normal Distributions_.  ACM Trans.  Math.  Software, 8, 2,
     June,163-179.

     To make use of this function, write first ‘load("distrib")’.


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function random_rayleigh b,  Next: Function random_student_t n,  Prev: Function random_poisson m,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: random_rayleigh (B)
     Returns a {\it Rayleigh}(b) random variate, with b>0.  Calling
     ‘random_rayleigh’ with a second argument _n_, a random sample of
     size _n_ will be simulated.

     The implemented algorithm is based on the general inverse method.

     To make use of this function, write first ‘load("distrib")’.


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function random_student_t n,  Next: Function random_weibull a b,  Prev: Function random_rayleigh b,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: random_student_t (N)
     Returns a Student random variate t(n) , with n>0.  Calling
     ‘random_student_t’ with a second argument _m_, a random sample of
     size _m_ will be simulated.

     The implemented algorithm is based on the fact that if Z is a
     normal random variable {\it Normal}(0, 1) and S^2 is a \chi^2
     random variable with n degrees of freedom, \chi^2(n) , then

     X={{Z}\over{\sqrt{{S^2}\over{n}}}}

     is a Student random variable with n degrees of freedom, t(n) .

     To make use of this function, write first ‘load("distrib")’.


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function random_weibull a b,  Next: Function skewness_bernoulli p,  Prev: Function random_student_t n,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: random_weibull (A, B)
     Returns a {\it Weibull}(a,b) random variate, with a,b>0.  Calling
     ‘random_weibull’ with a third argument _n_, a random sample of size
     _n_ will be simulated.

     The implemented algorithm is based on the general inverse method.

     To make use of this function, write first ‘load("distrib")’.


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function skewness_bernoulli p,  Next: Function skewness_beta a b,  Prev: Function random_weibull a b,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: skewness_bernoulli (P)
     Returns the skewness coefficient of a {\it Bernoulli}(p) random
     variable, with 0 \leq p \leq 1.

     The {\it Bernoulli}(p) random variable is equivalent to the {\it
     Binomial}(1,p) .

     The skewness coefficient is

     SK[X] = {1-2p \over \sqrt{p(1-p)}}

     (%i1) load ("distrib")$
     
     (%i2) skewness_bernoulli(p);
                                  1 - 2 p
     (%o2)                    ---------------
                              sqrt((1 - p) p)


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function skewness_beta a b,  Next: Function skewness_binomial n p,  Prev: Function skewness_bernoulli p,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: skewness_beta (A, B)
     Returns the skewness coefficient of a {\it Beta}(a,b) random
     variable, with a,b>0.  To make use of this function, write first
     ‘load("distrib")’.

     The skewness coefficient is

     SK[X] = {2(b-a)\sqrt{a+b+1} \over (a+b+2)\sqrt{ab}}


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function skewness_binomial n p,  Next: Function skewness_chi2 n,  Prev: Function skewness_beta a b,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: skewness_binomial (N, P)
     Returns the skewness coefficient of a {\it Binomial}(n,p) random
     variable, with 0 \leq p \leq 1 and n a positive integer.  To make
     use of this function, write first ‘load("distrib")’.

     The skewness coefficient is

     SK[X] = {1-2p\over \sqrt{np(1-p)}}


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function skewness_chi2 n,  Next: Function skewness_continuous_uniform a b,  Prev: Function skewness_binomial n p,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: skewness_chi2 (N)
     Returns the skewness coefficient of a Chi-square random variable
     \chi^2(n) , with n>0.

     The \chi^2(n) random variable is equivalent to the
     \Gamma\left(n/2,2\right) .

     The skewness coefficient is

     SK[X] = \sqrt{8\over n}

     (%i1) load ("distrib")$
     
     (%i2) skewness_chi2(n);
                                    3/2
                                   2
     (%o2)                        -------
                                  sqrt(n)


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function skewness_continuous_uniform a b,  Next: Function skewness_discrete_uniform n,  Prev: Function skewness_chi2 n,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: skewness_continuous_uniform (A, B)
     Returns the skewness coefficient of a {\it ContinuousUniform}(a,b)
     random variable, with a \lt b. To make use of this function, write
     first ‘load("distrib")’.

     The skewness coefficient is

     SK[X] = 0


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function skewness_discrete_uniform n,  Next: Function skewness_exp m,  Prev: Function skewness_continuous_uniform a b,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: skewness_discrete_uniform (N)
     Returns the skewness coefficient of a {\it DiscreteUniform}(n)
     random variable, with n a strictly positive integer.  To make use
     of this function, write first ‘load("distrib")’.

     The skewness coefficient is

     SK[X] = 0


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function skewness_exp m,  Next: Function skewness_f m n,  Prev: Function skewness_discrete_uniform n,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: skewness_exp (M)
     Returns the skewness coefficient of an {\it Exponential}(m) random
     variable, with m>0.

     The {\it Exponential}(m) random variable is equivalent to the {\it
     Weibull}(1,1/m) .

     The skewness coefficient is

     SK[X] = 2

     (%i1) load ("distrib")$
     
     (%i2) skewness_exp(m);
     (%o2)                           2


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function skewness_f m n,  Next: Function skewness_gamma a b,  Prev: Function skewness_exp m,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: skewness_f (M, N)
     Returns the skewness coefficient of a F random variable F(m,n),
     with m>0, n>6.  To make use of this function, write first
     ‘load("distrib")’.

     The skewness coefficient is

     SK[X] = {(n+2m-2)\sqrt{8(n-4)} \over (n-6)\sqrt{m(n+m-2)}}


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function skewness_gamma a b,  Next: Function skewness_general_finite_discrete v,  Prev: Function skewness_f m n,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: skewness_gamma (A, B)
     Returns the skewness coefficient of a \Gamma\left(a,b\right) random
     variable, with a,b>0.  To make use of this function, write first
     ‘load("distrib")’.

     The skewness coefficient is

     SK[X] = {2\over \sqrt{a}}


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function skewness_general_finite_discrete v,  Next: Function skewness_geometric p,  Prev: Function skewness_gamma a b,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: skewness_general_finite_discrete (V)
     Returns the skewness coefficient of a general finite discrete
     random variable, with vector probabilities v.

     See ‘pdf_general_finite_discrete’ for more details.

     See also: ‘pdf_general_finite_discrete’.


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function skewness_geometric p,  Next: Function skewness_gumbel a b,  Prev: Function skewness_general_finite_discrete v,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: skewness_geometric (P)
     Returns the skewness coefficient of a {\it Geometric}(p) random
     variable, with 0 < p \leq 1.

     The skewness coefficient is

     SK[X] = {2-p \over \sqrt{1-p}}

     ‘load("distrib")’ loads this function.


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function skewness_gumbel a b,  Next: Function skewness_hypergeometric n_1 n_2 n,  Prev: Function skewness_geometric p,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: skewness_gumbel (A, B)
     Returns the skewness coefficient of a {\it Gumbel}(a,b) random
     variable, with b>0.

     The skewness coefficient is

     SK[X] = {12\sqrt{6}\over \pi^3} \zeta(3)

     (%i1) load ("distrib")$
     
     (%i2) skewness_gumbel(a,b);
                                 3/2
                              2 6    zeta(3)
     (%o2)                    --------------
                                      3
                                   %pi

     where ‘zeta’ stands for the Riemann's zeta function.


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function skewness_hypergeometric n_1 n_2 n,  Next: Function skewness_laplace a b,  Prev: Function skewness_gumbel a b,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: skewness_hypergeometric (N_1, N_2, N)
     Returns the skewness coefficient of a {\it Hypergeometric}(n1,n2,n)
     random variable, with n_1, n_2 and n non negative integers and
     n\leq n1+n2.  To make use of this function, write first
     ‘load("distrib")’.

     The skewness coefficient is

     SK[X] = {(n_2-n_2)(n_1+n_2-2n)\over n_1+n_2-2} \sqrt{n_1+n_2-1
     \over n n_1 n_2 (n_1+n_2-n)}


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function skewness_laplace a b,  Next: Function skewness_logistic a b,  Prev: Function skewness_hypergeometric n_1 n_2 n,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: skewness_laplace (A, B)
     Returns the skewness coefficient of a {\it Laplace}(a,b) random
     variable, with b>0.  To make use of this function, write first
     ‘load("distrib")’.

     The skewness coefficient is

     SK[X] = 0


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function skewness_logistic a b,  Next: Function skewness_lognormal m s,  Prev: Function skewness_laplace a b,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: skewness_logistic (A, B)
     Returns the skewness coefficient of a {\it Logistic}(a,b) random
     variable, with b>0.  To make use of this function, write first
     ‘load("distrib")’.

     The skewness coefficient is

     SK[X] = 0


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function skewness_lognormal m s,  Next: Function skewness_negative_binomial n p,  Prev: Function skewness_logistic a b,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: skewness_lognormal (M, S)
     Returns the skewness coefficient of a {\it Lognormal}(m,s) random
     variable, with s>0.  To make use of this function, write first
     ‘load("distrib")’.

     The skewness coefficient is

     SK[X] =
     \left(\exp\left(s^2\right)+2\right)\sqrt{\exp\left(s^2\right)-1}


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function skewness_negative_binomial n p,  Next: Function skewness_noncentral_chi2 n ncp,  Prev: Function skewness_lognormal m s,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: skewness_negative_binomial (N, P)
     Returns the skewness coefficient of a {\it NegativeBinomial}(n,p)
     random variable, with 0 < p \leq 1 and n a positive number.  To
     make use of this function, write first ‘load("distrib")’.

     The skewness coefficient is

     SK[X] = {2-p \over \sqrt{n(1-p)}}


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function skewness_noncentral_chi2 n ncp,  Next: Function skewness_noncentral_student_t n ncp,  Prev: Function skewness_negative_binomial n p,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: skewness_noncentral_chi2 (N, NCP)
     Returns the skewness coefficient of a noncentral Chi-square random
     variable m4_noncentral_chi2(n,ncp) , with n>0 and noncentrality
     parameter ncp \ge 0.

     The skewness coefficient is

     SK[X] = {2^{3/2}(n+3\mu) \over (n+2\mu)^{3/2}}

     where \mu is the noncentrality parameter _ncp_.


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function skewness_noncentral_student_t n ncp,  Next: Function skewness_normal m s,  Prev: Function skewness_noncentral_chi2 n ncp,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: skewness_noncentral_student_t (N, NCP)
     Returns the skewness coefficient of a noncentral Student random
     variable {\it nc\_t}(n, ncp) , with n>3 degrees of freedom and
     noncentrality parameter ncp.  To make use of this function, write
     first ‘load("distrib")’.

     If U is a non-central Student's t random variable with n degrees of
     freedom and a noncentrality parameter \mu, the skewness is

     \begin{aligned} SK[U] &=
     {\mu\sqrt{n}\,\Gamma\left({{n-1}\over{2}}\right)
     \over{\sqrt{2}\Gamma\left({{n
     }\over{2}}\right)\sigma^{3}}}\left({{n
     \left(2n+\mu^2-3\right)}\over{\left(n-3\right)\left(n-2\right)}}
     -2\sigma^2\right) \cr \sigma^2 &=
     {{n\left(\mu^2+1\right)}\over{n-2}}-{{n \mu^2\,
     \Gamma\left({{n-1}\over{2}}\right)^2}\over{2\Gamma\left({{n
     }\over{2}}\right)^2}} \end{aligned}


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function skewness_normal m s,  Next: Function skewness_pareto a b,  Prev: Function skewness_noncentral_student_t n ncp,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: skewness_normal (M, S)
     Returns the skewness coefficient of a {\it Normal}(m, s) random
     variable, with s>0.  To make use of this function, write first
     ‘load("distrib")’.

     The skewness coefficient is

     SK[X] = 0


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function skewness_pareto a b,  Next: Function skewness_poisson m,  Prev: Function skewness_normal m s,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: skewness_pareto (A, B)
     Returns the skewness coefficient of a {\it Pareto}(a,b) random
     variable, with a>3,b>0.  To make use of this function, write first
     ‘load("distrib")’.

     The skewness coefficient is

     SK[X] = {2(a+1)\over a-3} \sqrt{a-2\over a}


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function skewness_poisson m,  Next: Function skewness_rayleigh b,  Prev: Function skewness_pareto a b,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: skewness_poisson (M)
     Returns the skewness coefficient of a {\it Poisson}(m) random
     variable, with m>0.  To make use of this function, write first
     ‘load("distrib")’.

     The skewness is

     SK[X] = {1\over \sqrt{m}}


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function skewness_rayleigh b,  Next: Function skewness_student_t n,  Prev: Function skewness_poisson m,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: skewness_rayleigh (B)
     Returns the skewness coefficient of a {\it Rayleigh}(b) random
     variable, with b>0.

     The {\it Rayleigh}(b) random variable is equivalent to the {\it
     Weibull}(2,1/b) .

     The skewness coefficient is

     SK[X] = {2\sqrt{\pi}(\pi - 3)\over (4-\pi)^{3/2}}

     (%i1) load ("distrib")$
     
     (%i2) skewness_rayleigh(b);
                              3/2
                           %pi      3 sqrt(%pi)
                           ------ - -----------
                             4           4
     (%o2)                 --------------------
                                    %pi 3/2
                               (1 - ---)
                                     4


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function skewness_student_t n,  Next: Function skewness_weibull a b,  Prev: Function skewness_rayleigh b,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: skewness_student_t (N)
     Returns the skewness coefficient of a Student random variable t(n)
     , with n>3, which is always equal to 0.  To make use of this
     function, write first ‘load("distrib")’.

     The skewness coefficient is

     SK[X] = 0


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function skewness_weibull a b,  Next: Function std_bernoulli p,  Prev: Function skewness_student_t n,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: skewness_weibull (A, B)
     Returns the skewness coefficient of a {\it Weibull}(a,b) random
     variable, with a,b>0.  To make use of this function, write first
     ‘load("distrib")’.

     The skewness coefficient is

     SK[X] = {\displaystyle\Gamma\left(1+{3\over a}\right)
     -3\Gamma\left(1+{1\over a}\right)\Gamma\left(1+{2\over
     a}\right)+2\Gamma\left(1+{1\over a}\right)^3 \over
     \displaystyle\left[\Gamma\left(1+{2\over
     a}\right)-\Gamma\left(1+{1\over a}\right)^2\right]^{3/2} }


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function std_bernoulli p,  Next: Function std_beta a b,  Prev: Function skewness_weibull a b,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: std_bernoulli (P)
     Returns the standard deviation of a {\it Bernoulli}(p) random
     variable, with 0 \leq p \leq 1.

     The {\it Bernoulli}(p) random variable is equivalent to the {\it
     Binomial}(1,p) .

     The standard deviation is

     D[X] = \sqrt{p(1-p)}

     (%i1) load ("distrib")$
     
     (%i2) std_bernoulli(p);
     (%o2)                    sqrt((1 - p) p)


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function std_beta a b,  Next: Function std_binomial n p,  Prev: Function std_bernoulli p,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: std_beta (A, B)
     Returns the standard deviation of a {\it Beta}(a,b) random
     variable, with a,b>0.  To make use of this function, write first
     ‘load("distrib")’.

     The standard deviation is

     D[X] = {1\over a+b}\sqrt{ab\over a+b+1}


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function std_binomial n p,  Next: Function std_chi2 n,  Prev: Function std_beta a b,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: std_binomial (N, P)
     Returns the standard deviation of a {\it Binomial}(n,p) random
     variable, with 0 \leq p \leq 1 and n a positive integer.  To make
     use of this function, write first ‘load("distrib")’.

     The standard deviation is

     D[X] = \sqrt{np(1-p)}


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function std_chi2 n,  Next: Function std_continuous_uniform a b,  Prev: Function std_binomial n p,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: std_chi2 (N)
     Returns the standard deviation of a Chi-square random variable
     \chi^2(n) , with n>0.

     The \chi^2(n) random variable is equivalent to the
     \Gamma\left(n/2,2\right) .

     The standard deviation is

     D[X] = \sqrt{2n}

     (%i1) load ("distrib")$
     
     (%i2) std_chi2(n);
     (%o2)                    sqrt(2) sqrt(n)


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function std_continuous_uniform a b,  Next: Function std_discrete_uniform n,  Prev: Function std_chi2 n,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: std_continuous_uniform (A, B)
     Returns the standard deviation of a {\it ContinuousUniform}(a,b)
     random variable, with a \lt b. To make use of this function, write
     first ‘load("distrib")’.

     The standard deviation is

     D[X] = {b-a \over 2\sqrt{3}}


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function std_discrete_uniform n,  Next: Function std_exp m,  Prev: Function std_continuous_uniform a b,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: std_discrete_uniform (N)
     Returns the standard deviation of a {\it DiscreteUniform}(n) random
     variable, with n a strictly positive integer.  To make use of this
     function, write first ‘load("distrib")’.

     The standard deviation is

     D[X] = {\sqrt{n^2-1} \over 2\sqrt{3}}


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function std_exp m,  Next: Function std_f m n,  Prev: Function std_discrete_uniform n,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: std_exp (M)
     Returns the standard deviation of an {\it Exponential}(m) random
     variable, with m>0.

     The {\it Exponential}(m) random variable is equivalent to the {\it
     Weibull}(1,1/m) .

     The standard deviation is

     D[X] = {1\over m}

     (%i1) load ("distrib")$
     
     (%i2) std_exp(m);
                                     1
     (%o2)                           -
                                     m


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function std_f m n,  Next: Function std_gamma a b,  Prev: Function std_exp m,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: std_f (M, N)
     Returns the standard deviation of a F random variable F(m,n), with
     m>0, n>4.  To make use of this function, write first
     ‘load("distrib")’.

     The standard deviation is

     D[X] = {\sqrt{2}\, n \over n-2} \sqrt{n+m-2\over m(n-4)}


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function std_gamma a b,  Next: Function std_general_finite_discrete v,  Prev: Function std_f m n,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: std_gamma (A, B)
     Returns the standard deviation of a \Gamma\left(a,b\right) random
     variable, with a,b>0.  To make use of this function, write first
     ‘load("distrib")’.

     The standard deviation is

     D[X] = b\sqrt{a}


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function std_general_finite_discrete v,  Next: Function std_geometric p,  Prev: Function std_gamma a b,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: std_general_finite_discrete (V)
     Returns the standard deviation of a general finite discrete random
     variable, with vector probabilities v.

     See ‘pdf_general_finite_discrete’ for more details.

     See also: ‘pdf_general_finite_discrete’.


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function std_geometric p,  Next: Function std_gumbel a b,  Prev: Function std_general_finite_discrete v,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: std_geometric (P)
     Returns the standard deviation of a {\it Geometric}(p) random
     variable, with 0 < p \leq 1.

     D[X] = {\sqrt{1-p} \over p}

     ‘load("distrib")’ loads this function.


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function std_gumbel a b,  Next: Function std_hypergeometric n_1 n_2 n,  Prev: Function std_geometric p,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: std_gumbel (A, B)
     Returns the standard deviation of a {\it Gumbel}(a,b) random
     variable, with b>0.  To make use of this function, write first
     ‘load("distrib")’.

     The standard deviation is

     D[X] = {\pi \over \sqrt{6}} b


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function std_hypergeometric n_1 n_2 n,  Next: Function std_laplace a b,  Prev: Function std_gumbel a b,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: std_hypergeometric (N_1, N_2, N)
     Returns the standard deviation of a {\it Hypergeometric}(n_1,n_2,n)
     random variable, with n_1, n_2 and n non negative integers and
     n\leq n_1+n_2.  To make use of this function, write first
     ‘load("distrib")’.

     The standard deviation is

     D[X] = {1\over n_1+n_2}\sqrt{n n_1 n_2 (n_1 + n_2 - n) \over
     n_1+n_2-1}


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function std_laplace a b,  Next: Function std_logistic a b,  Prev: Function std_hypergeometric n_1 n_2 n,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: std_laplace (A, B)
     Returns the standard deviation of a {\it Laplace}(a,b) random
     variable, with b>0.  To make use of this function, write first
     ‘load("distrib")’.

     The standard deviation is

     D[X] = \sqrt{2} b


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function std_logistic a b,  Next: Function std_lognormal m s,  Prev: Function std_laplace a b,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: std_logistic (A, B)
     Returns the standard deviation of a {\it Logistic}(a,b) random
     variable , with b>0.  To make use of this function, write first
     ‘load("distrib")’.

     The standard deviation is

     D[X] = {\pi b\over \sqrt{3}}


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function std_lognormal m s,  Next: Function std_negative_binomial n p,  Prev: Function std_logistic a b,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: std_lognormal (M, S)
     Returns the standard deviation of a {\it Lognormal}(m,s) random
     variable, with s>0.  To make use of this function, write first
     ‘load("distrib")’.

     The standard deviation is

     D[X] = \sqrt{\left(\exp\left(s^2\right) - 1\right)}
     \exp\left(m+{s^2\over 2}\right)


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function std_negative_binomial n p,  Next: Function std_noncentral_chi2 n ncp,  Prev: Function std_lognormal m s,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: std_negative_binomial (N, P)
     Returns the standard deviation of a {\it NegativeBinomial}(n,p)
     random variable, with 0 < p \leq 1 and n a positive number.  To
     make use of this function, write first ‘load("distrib")’.

     The standard deviation is

     D[X] = {\sqrt{n(1-p)}\over p}


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function std_noncentral_chi2 n ncp,  Next: Function std_noncentral_student_t n ncp,  Prev: Function std_negative_binomial n p,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: std_noncentral_chi2 (N, NCP)
     Returns the standard deviation of a noncentral Chi-square random
     variable m4_noncentral_chi2(n,ncp) , with n>0 and noncentrality
     parameter ncp \ge 0.

     The standard deviation is

     D[X] = \sqrt{2(n+2\mu)}

     where \mu is the noncentrality parameter _ncp_.


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function std_noncentral_student_t n ncp,  Next: Function std_normal m s,  Prev: Function std_noncentral_chi2 n ncp,  Up: distrib

 -- Function: std_noncentral_student_t (N, NCP)
     Returns the standard deviation of a noncentral Student random
     variable {\it nc\_t}(n, ncp) , with n>2 degrees of freedom and
     noncentrality parameter ncp.  To make use of this function, write
     first ‘load("distrib")’.

     The standard deviation is

     D[X] = \sqrt{{n(\mu^2+1)\over n-2} - {n\mu^2\;
     \Gamma\left(\displaystyle{n-1\over 2}\right)^2 \over
     2\Gamma\left(\displaystyle{n\over 2}\right)^2}}


File: maxima-core-docs.info,  Node: Function std_normal m s,  Next: Function std_pareto a b,  Prev: Function std_noncentral_student_t n ncp,  Up: distrib

